In article <1991Jan12.004100.24536@NCoast.ORG> allbery@ncoast.ORG (Brandon S. Allbery KB8JRR) writes:
>
>I wasn't on the Mac dev team, but I have programmed for the Mac.
>
>The Mac (and other systems like the Amiga and even MS Windows to some extent)
>prefers to use small relocatable segments instead of one monolithic chunk of
>code or data
So far so good.

>  Memory compaction can cause these segments to move
>around while the program is running --- where Unix uses pointers, the three
>aforementioned systems use "handles", or double-indirect pointers.  The handle
>remains constant, but its value (the pointer to the data) can be changed when
>the segment the handle points into is not locked.
Not on the Amiga.
The Amiga uses scatter loading, but once memory is allocated, it's not reused,
moved, garbage collected, or anything else until it is explicitly released
by the program (if it's never relased, it's lost).  The up side to this is that
programs run faster (no flames please on how little effect this actually is);
the down side is that fragmentation issues come into play - it can be
impossible to load a program even if there is enough free memory because the
largest free block is too small and there isn't any way to combine the
free areas (automatically).  This is also one reason that memory leaks are a
bad thing.

>
>What this means that things can move around in memory, not only between
>NetHack sessions, but also in the middle of one.
For the Amiga, this is only an issue between sessions.

